### Why is confirming before commit dangerous?

A payment is not real until the transaction that moves money and records it has durably committed. Before that, many things can still make it fail: insufficient funds, a constraint violation, a fraud check rejection, a dropped connection, or a crash that rolls the transaction back.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class DangerousPaymentService {

    // WRONG: returns "confirmed" before commit
    public PaymentResult processWrong(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Optimistically tell the UI "confirmed"
        uiNotifier.send(accountId, "Payment confirmed!"); // <-- LIE

        try {
            accountRepo.debit(accountId, amount); // May fail!
            // Commit happens here
        } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
            // Oops: already told user it was confirmed
            uiNotifier.send(accountId, "Actually, payment failed.");
            // User may have already acted on the false confirmation!
        }
        return PaymentResult.success();
    }
}
```

```text
Danger timeline:
  t=0ms:   UI shows "Payment confirmed!" (before commit)
  t=50ms:  Rajesh sees confirmation, closes app
  t=100ms: Database check: insufficient funds!
  t=101ms: Transaction rolled back
  Result:  Rajesh believes he paid. Bank says he did not.
           Reconciliation nightmare.
```

### What are the real-world consequences?

The cost of a false positive in payments is severe and asymmetric. A wrongly-shown confirmation can lead to irreversible actions.

```text
Consequences of premature confirmation:
  1. Rajesh thinks bill is paid -> stops following up -> late fees
  2. Merchant sees "confirmed" -> ships goods -> no money arrives
  3. Priya transfers to friend -> friend sees "received" -> spends it
     -> rollback -> friend's account goes negative
  4. Audit trail shows confirmation at time T but no commit
     -> compliance violation

  Cost of honest "pending": slightly slower perceived UX
  Cost of false "confirmed": support tickets, reconciliation,
                              compliance issues, lost trust
```

### How should fast payments work?

For fast synchronous payments, confirm only after the durable commit returns success.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class CorrectPaymentService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public CorrectPaymentService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public PaymentResult process(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Perform the charge
        accountRepo.debit(accountId, amount);
        // Transaction COMMITS here (end of @Transactional)
        // Only AFTER commit: return success
        return PaymentResult.confirmed(accountId, amount);
    }

    // If commit fails, exception propagates, UI shows error
    // Never shows "confirmed" unless money actually moved
}
```

```text
Correct flow:
  t=0ms:   UI shows spinner
  t=50ms:  Database commit succeeds
  t=51ms:  API returns 200 {"status": "CONFIRMED"}
  t=52ms:  UI shows "Payment confirmed!"
  Guarantee: confirmation = committed

  If commit fails:
  t=50ms:  Database rollback
  t=51ms:  API returns 400 {"error": "Insufficient funds"}
  t=52ms:  UI shows "Payment failed: insufficient funds"
  Guarantee: user never sees false confirmation
```

### How should long-running payments work?

For slow work involving external gateways, return 202 Accepted with an explicit PENDING state. The confirmation transitions only when the durable commit of the final step happens.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.net.URI;

@RestController
public class LongRunningPaymentController {

    private final PaymentJobService jobService;

    public LongRunningPaymentController(PaymentJobService jobService) {
        this.jobService = jobService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/api/payments/international")
    public ResponseEntity<PaymentJob> pay(@RequestBody PaymentRequest req) {
        // Fast: validate + persist PENDING + enqueue
        PaymentJob job = jobService.accept(req);

        return ResponseEntity
            .status(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED)
            .location(URI.create("/api/payments/" + job.getId() + "/status"))
            .body(job);
        // UI shows: "Payment processing..."
        // NOT "Payment confirmed!"
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class PaymentWorker {

    private final PaymentJobRepository jobRepo;
    private final SwiftGateway swiftGateway;
    private final NotificationService notifications;

    public PaymentWorker(PaymentJobRepository jobRepo,
                          SwiftGateway swiftGateway,
                          NotificationService notifications) {
        this.jobRepo = jobRepo;
        this.swiftGateway = swiftGateway;
        this.notifications = notifications;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void process(String jobId) {
        PaymentJob job = jobRepo.findById(jobId);

        try {
            swiftGateway.send(job);
            // SWIFT confirms -> commit CONFIRMED status
            job.setStatus(PaymentStatus.CONFIRMED);
            jobRepo.save(job);
            // NOW notify: confirmation driven by committed outcome
            notifications.send(job.getAccountId(),
                "Payment confirmed: " + job.getAmount());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            job.setStatus(PaymentStatus.FAILED);
            jobRepo.save(job);
            notifications.send(job.getAccountId(),
                "Payment failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Long-running flow:
  t=0ms:   UI shows "Processing..." (PENDING)
  t=30s:   SWIFT confirms
  t=30.1s: Database commits CONFIRMED status
  t=30.2s: Push notification: "Payment confirmed!"
  Guarantee: confirmation always after durable commit
```

### How should the status transitions work?

The status transitions from PENDING to CONFIRMED only when the durable commit happens, and to FAILED if it does not.

```text
Status state machine:
  PENDING ──> PROCESSING ──> CONFIRMED (durable commit succeeded)
                          ──> FAILED    (commit failed or rolled back)

  Rules:
  - PENDING shown to user immediately (202 response)
  - PROCESSING shown during background work
  - CONFIRMED only after durable commit of final step
  - FAILED on any rollback or rejection
  - Transition to CONFIRMED is idempotent
    (duplicate webhook cannot flip FAILED to CONFIRMED)
```

### Why must the transition be idempotent?

The confirmation must be derived from the committed record, not from an in-memory flag, so a replay or duplicate notification cannot flip a failed payment to confirmed.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class IdempotentStatusTransition {

    private final PaymentJobRepository jobRepo;

    public IdempotentStatusTransition(PaymentJobRepository jobRepo) {
        this.jobRepo = jobRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void confirmIfPending(String jobId) {
        // Conditional update: only transition from PROCESSING
        int updated = jobRepo.updateStatusConditional(
            jobId, PaymentStatus.PROCESSING, PaymentStatus.CONFIRMED);

        if (updated == 0) {
            // Already confirmed or failed: no-op (idempotent)
            System.out.println("Job " + jobId +
                " not in PROCESSING state, skipping");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotent transition:
  Webhook 1: confirmIfPending("JOB-123") -> PROCESSING -> CONFIRMED
  Webhook 2: confirmIfPending("JOB-123") -> already CONFIRMED -> no-op
  Duplicate webhook cannot flip FAILED to CONFIRMED
```

### What should you tell the product manager?

Explain the asymmetric cost: a false confirmation has expensive real-world consequences, while an honest pending state with a fast 202 response still feels responsive.

| Approach | UX Speed | Correctness | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| Confirm before commit | Instant | Wrong | False positives, support tickets |
| Confirm after commit | ~50ms slower | Correct | None |
| 202 + pending (long work) | Instant ack | Correct | None |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is sending a confirmation notification from an event handler that fires before the transaction commits.

```text
WRONG: @TransactionalEventListener(phase = BEFORE_COMMIT)
  Fires notification before commit -> commit may still fail
  User sees "confirmed" -> then gets "actually failed"

CORRECT: @TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)
  Fires notification ONLY after commit succeeds
  User sees "confirmed" only when money has actually moved
```
